            },
            "raw_output": "1\t{\n2\t  \"messages\": [\n3\t    {\n4\t      \"time\": 1789500108.141697,\n5\t      \"type\": \"msg\",\n6\t      \"sender\": \"kwebster\",\n7\t      \"metadata\": {\n8\t        \"to_persona\": \"jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona\",\n9\t        \"settings\": {},\n10\t        \"model\": {\n11\t          \"settings\": {},\n12\t          \"id\": \"sonnet\"\n13\t        }\n14\t      },\n15\t      \"id\": \"5a161bec-9667-46ac-b59d-8715a8cacec7\",\n16\t      \"raw_time\": false,\n17\t      \"body\": \"I want to use the Surya architecture to test it's abilities against the baseline of hemispheric chirality of filaments. I understand that the baseline already scores really well since hemispheres are highly associated with certain chiralities. I have a catalog of 45 filament events and they are labeled with hemisphere (north or south) and I also know the chirality of them (dextral or sinestral). I would like to come up with a baseline to use in the surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/1_baseline_template.ipynb notebook.\"\n18\t    },\n19\t    {\n20\t      \"raw_time\": false,\n21\t      \"body\": \"I'll look at the existing structure to understand the baseline pattern used elsewhere in the repo before building yours.\",\n22\t      \"time\": 1789500115.2814105,\n23\t      \"id\": \"a35e5e0b-2bfb-4043-88dd-897a300e6bdf\",\n24\t      \"mentions\": [],\n25\t      \"sender\": \"jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona\",\n26\t      \"type\": \"msg\"\n27\t    },\n28\t    {\n29\t      \"body\": \"\",\n30\t      \"raw_time\": false,\n31\t      \"type\": \"msg\",\n32\t      \"metadata\": {\n33\t        \"tool_calls\": [\n34\t          {\n35\t            \"tool_call_id\": \"toolu_bdrk_01TPnbfqYoFGxvGpuvYEUUM2\",\n36\t            \"raw_input\": {\n37\t              \"description\": \"List filament_kyle app directory contents\",\n38\t              \"command\": \"find /home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle -maxdepth 3 | sort\"\n39\t            },\n40\t            \"status\": \"completed\",\n41\t            \"raw_output\": \"/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/.ipynb_checkpoints\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/.ipynb_checkpoints/0_dataset_dataloader_template-checkpoint.ipynb\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/1_baseline_template.ipynb\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/2_finetune_template_1D.ipynb\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/3_finetune_template_1D.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/ADAPTING.md\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/A_shapes_and_broadcasting.ipynb\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/__pycache__\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/__pycache__/configs.cpython-312.pyc\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/assets\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/assets/.cache\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/assets/.cache/huggingface\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/assets/.gitkeep\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/assets/scalers.yaml\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/brainstorming.chat\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/configs\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/configs.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/configs/.ipynb_checkpoints\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/configs/config_script.yaml\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/configs/hemisphere_loader.chat\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/data\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/data/.ipynb_checkpoints\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/data/.ipynb_checkpoints/fil_data-checkpoint.csv\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/data/.ipynb_checkpoints/hek_flare_catalog-checkpoint.csv\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/data/fil_data.csv\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/data/hek_flare_catalog.csv\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/datasets\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/datasets/.ipynb_checkpoints\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/datasets/__init__.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/datasets/__pycache__\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/datasets/__pycache__/__init__.cpython-312.pyc\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/datasets/__pycache__/filament_dataset.cpython-312.pyc\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/datasets/filament_dataset.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/download_scalers.sh\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/download_scalers_and_weights.sh\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/lightning_modules\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/lightning_modules/__init__.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/lightning_modules/pl_simple_baseline.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/logs\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/logs/data\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/logs/data/20260914T180019Z_000_data_train_3616.log\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/logs/data/20260914T183047Z_000_data_train_3616.log\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/logs/data/20260915T153427Z_000_data_train_1029.log\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/metrics\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/metrics/__init__.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/metrics/template_metrics.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/models\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/models/__init__.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/models/simple_baseline.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/untitled.chat\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/untitled1.chat\",\n42\t            \"kind\": \"execute\",\n43\t            \"title\": \"find filament_kyle -maxdepth 3 | sort\"\n44\t          },\n45\t          {\n46\t            \"permission_status\": \"resolved\",\n47\t            \"selected_option_id\": \"allow-with-updates\",\n48\t            \"permission_options\": [\n49\t              {\n50\t                \"option_id\": \"allow-once\",\n51\t                \"name\": \"Yes\",\n52\t                \"kind\": \"allow_once\"\n53\t              },\n54\t              {\n55\t                \"option_id\": \"allow-with-updates\",\n56\t                \"name\": \"Yes, and allow access to template/ and similar commands\",\n57\t                \"kind\": \"allow_always\"\n58\t              },\n59\t              {\n60\t                \"name\": \"No\",\n61\t                \"kind\": \"reject_once\",\n62\t                \"option_id\": \"reject\"\n63\t              }\n64\t            ],\n65\t            \"session_id\": \"82699d0c-9c18-4369-8dff-5a5039067424\",\n66\t            \"title\": \"find template -maxdepth 3 | sort\",\n67\t            \"kind\": \"execute\",\n68\t            \"status\": \"completed\",\n69\t            \"tool_call_id\": \"toolu_bdrk_01Lji4KswdoWGV4ndJzbeZiW\",\n70\t            \"raw_input\": {\n71\t              \"description\": \"List template app directory contents for comparison\",\n72\t              \"command\": \"find /home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template -maxdepth 3 | sort\"\n73\t            },\n74\t            \"raw_output\": \"/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/.ipynb_checkpoints\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/.ipynb_checkpoints/0_dataset_dataloader_template-checkpoint.ipynb\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/1_baseline_template.ipynb\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/2_finetune_template_1D.ipynb\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/3_finetune_template_1D.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/ADAPTING.md\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/A_shapes_and_broadcasting.ipynb\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/__pycache__\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/__pycache__/configs.cpython-312.pyc\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/assets\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/assets/.cache\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/assets/.cache/huggingface\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/assets/.gitkeep\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/assets/scalers.yaml\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/configs\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/configs.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/configs/.ipynb_checkpoints\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/configs/config_script.yaml\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/data\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/data/hek_flare_catalog.csv\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/datasets\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/datasets/.ipynb_checkpoints\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/datasets/__init__.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/datasets/__pycache__\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/datasets/__pycache__/__init__.cpython-312.pyc\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/datasets/__pycache__/template_dataset.cpython-312.pyc\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/datasets/template_dataset.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/download_scalers.sh\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/download_scalers_and_weights.sh\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/lightning_modules\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/lightning_modules/__init__.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/lightning_modules/pl_simple_baseline.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/metrics\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/metrics/__init__.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/metrics/template_metrics.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/models\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/models/__init__.py\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/template/models/simple_baseline.py\"\n75\t          },\n76\t          {\n77\t            \"raw_output\": \"--- fil_data.csv head ---\\n\\ufeffstart_time,hemisphere\\r\\n2012-03-05T04:00:05,1\\r\\n2012-03-07T00:24:06,1\\r\\n2012-03-07T01:30:06,1\\r\\n2012-03-13T17:36:05,1\\r\\n2012-04-23T18:24:05,1\\r\\n2012-06-14T14:12:07,0\\r\\n2012-07-12T16:48:05,0\\r\\n2012-09-28T00:12:05,1\\r\\n2012-03-15T07:12:05,1\\r\\n2013-07-11T15:12:10,0\\r\\n2015-03-15T01:48:05,0--- row count ---\\n11 data/fil_data.csv\",\n78\t            \"title\": \"echo \\\"--- fil_data.csv head ---\\\" && head -20 data/fil_data.csv && echo \\\"--- row count ---\\\" && wc -l data/fil_data.csv\",\n79\t            \"kind\": \"execute\",\n80\t            \"raw_input\": {\n81\t              \"description\": \"Inspect filament catalog CSV\",\n82\t              \"command\": \"echo \\\"--- fil_data.csv head ---\\\" && head -20 data/fil_data.csv && echo \\\"--- row count ---\\\" && wc -l data/fil_data.csv\"\n83\t            },\n84\t            \"status\": \"completed\",\n85\t            \"tool_call_id\": \"toolu_bdrk_0164UtXd4EYZmCVnD37Ky7xZ\"\n86\t          },\n87\t          {\n88\t            \"kind\": \"read\",\n89\t            \"status\": \"completed\",\n90\t            \"raw_output\": \"1\\t\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n2\\tTask-specific configuration for the flare-forecasting template app.\\n3\\t\\n4\\tEverything generic \\u2014 paths, channels, temporal sampling, S3 settings, the model and\\n5\\tLoRA configs, the training and logging sections, and ``load_config()`` itself \\u2014 lives in\\n6\\t``workshop_infrastructure/configs.py``. This file holds only what is specific to *this*\\n7\\ttask: the flare catalog and how its events are aligned to the Surya index.\\n8\\t\\n9\\t**This is the pattern to copy when you fork the template.** Subclass ``DataConfig`` with\\n10\\tyour task's fields, then bind ``load_config`` to it. You never maintain a copy of the\\n11\\tbase config.\\n12\\t\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n13\\t\\n14\\tfrom __future__ import annotations\\n15\\t\\n16\\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass\\n17\\tfrom functools import partial\\n18\\tfrom typing import ClassVar\\n19\\t\\n20\\tfrom workshop_infrastructure.configs import (  # re-exported for convenience\\n21\\t    DataConfig,\\n22\\t    LoraAdapterConfig,\\n23\\t    ModelConfig,\\n24\\t    OutputConfig,\\n25\\t    TimeEmbeddingConfig,\\n26\\t    TrainingConfig,\\n27\\t    load_config,\\n28\\t)\\n29\\t\\n30\\t\\n31\\t@dataclass\\n32\\tclass FilamentDataConfig(DataConfig):\\n33\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"DataConfig plus the filament-catalog alignment settings used by ``FilamentDataset``.\\n34\\t\\n35\\t    These four keys are what makes this app's ``data:`` section different from any other\\n36\\t    downstream task's. Swap them for your own when you fork.\\n37\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n38\\t    # Path to the label catalog (relative paths resolve against the config file's dir).\\n39\\t    filament_index_path: str = \\\"\\\"\\n40\\t    # Column in the catalog holding the event timestamp.\\n41\\t    ds_time_column: str = \\\"start_time\\\"\\n42\\t    # Max allowed gap when matching catalog events to Surya timesteps.\\n43\\t    ds_time_tolerance: str = \\\"2d\\\"\\n44\\t    # \\\"forward\\\" uses the solar state *before* the flare (causal prediction).\\n45\\t    ds_match_direction: str = \\\"forward\\\"\\n46\\t\\n47\\t    # flare_index_path is a path, so it must join the base class's list to get the same\\n48\\t    # relative-to-the-config-file resolution. Extend this whenever you add a path field.\\n49\\t    PATH_FIELDS: ClassVar[tuple[str, ...]] = DataConfig.PATH_FIELDS + (\\\"filament_index_path\\\",)\\n50\\t\\n51\\t\\n52\\t# The app's entry point. Identical to load_config() except that the data: section is\\n53\\t# parsed into FlareDataConfig, so the four keys above are recognized instead of rejected.\\n54\\tload_filament_config = partial(load_config, data_cls=FilamentDataConfig)\\n55\\t\\n56\\t\\n57\\t__all__ = [\\n58\\t    \\\"FilamentDataConfig\\\",\\n59\\t    \\\"load_filament_config\\\",\\n60\\t    # Re-exports so app code can import everything config-related from one place.\\n61\\t    \\\"DataConfig\\\",\\n62\\t    \\\"OutputConfig\\\",\\n63\\t    \\\"TrainingConfig\\\",\\n64\\t    \\\"ModelConfig\\\",\\n65\\t    \\\"LoraAdapterConfig\\\",\\n66\\t    \\\"TimeEmbeddingConfig\\\",\\n67\\t    \\\"load_config\\\",\\n68\\t]\\n69\\t\",\n91\t            \"tool_call_id\": \"toolu_bdrk_01PDRscruUWEcRzxo8Upw6Vg\",\n92\t            \"title\": \"Read configs.py\",\n93\t            \"raw_input\": {\n94\t              \"file_path\": \"/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/configs.py\"\n95\t            },\n96\t            \"locations\": [\n97\t              \"/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/configs.py\"\n98\t            ]\n99\t          },\n100\t          {\n101\t            \"raw_input\": {\n102\t              \"file_path\": \"/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/datasets/filament_dataset.py\"\n103\t            },\n104\t            \"kind\": \"read\",\n105\t            \"tool_call_id\": \"toolu_bdrk_01BJUtBJqQMUXDAbKoyXKp9K\",\n106\t            \"title\": \"Read datasets/filament_dataset.py\",\n107\t            \"status\": \"completed\",\n108\t            \"raw_output\": \"1\\timport numpy as np\\n2\\timport pandas as pd\\n3\\tfrom typing import Callable, Literal\\n4\\tfrom workshop_infrastructure.datasets.helio import HelioNetCDFDataset\\n5\\t\\n6\\t\\n7\\tclass FilamentDataset(HelioNetCDFDataset):\\n8\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n9\\t    Template child class of HelioNetCDFDataset showing how to build a downstream dataset.\\n10\\t    Extends the base class with a flare intensity label aligned to the Surya index.\\n11\\t\\n12\\t    All ``HelioNetCDFDataset`` keyword arguments (``index_path``, ``scalers``, ``channels``,\\n13\\t    ``s3_cache_dir``, etc.) are accepted via ``**kwargs`` and forwarded to the base class.\\n14\\t    ``load_forecast_frames`` defaults to ``False`` here (flare forecasting supplies its own\\n15\\t    labels, so future Surya frames are never fetched); pass it explicitly to override.\\n16\\t\\n17\\t    Additional Args:\\n18\\t        return_surya_stack: If True (default), include the Surya image stack in the returned dict.\\n19\\t            Set to False to return only the flare intensity label (useful for label inspection).\\n20\\t        max_number_of_samples: Cap the dataset length at this value. Useful for quick experiments.\\n21\\t        label_transform: Optional callable applied to the ``intensity`` column of the flare index\\n22\\t            to produce the ``normalized_intensity`` label. Signature:\\n23\\t            ``(series: pd.Series) -> pd.Series``.  If ``None``, the raw intensity values are\\n24\\t            used as-is. Define this at the call site (e.g., in ``build_datasets()``) to keep\\n25\\t            normalization logic out of the dataset class.\\n26\\t        flare_index_path: Path to the downstream flare intensity CSV index.\\n27\\t        ds_time_column: Column name in the flare index to use as the event timestamp.\\n28\\t        ds_time_tolerance: Maximum allowed time offset when matching Surya and DS indices\\n29\\t            (e.g., ``\\\"15min\\\"``). Unmatched entries are dropped.\\n30\\t        ds_match_direction: Merge direction passed to ``pd.merge_asof``. Use ``\\\"forward\\\"``\\n31\\t            for causal prediction (predict flares from prior solar state).\\n32\\t\\n33\\t    Raises:\\n34\\t        ValueError: If ``filament_index_path`` is not provided, or if no overlap exists\\n35\\t            between the Surya and DS indices within the specified tolerance.\\n36\\t    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n37\\t\\n38\\t    def __init__(\\n39\\t        self,\\n40\\t        # Downstream-specific parameters\\n41\\t        return_surya_stack: bool = True,\\n42\\t        max_number_of_samples: int | None = None,\\n43\\t        label_transform: Callable[[pd.Series], pd.Series] | None = None,\\n44\\t        filament_index_path: str | None = None,\\n45\\t        ds_time_column: str | None = None,\\n46\\t        ds_time_tolerance: str | None = None,\\n47\\t        ds_match_direction: Literal[\\\"forward\\\", \\\"backward\\\", \\\"nearest\\\"] = \\\"forward\\\",\\n48\\t        # All HelioNetCDFDataset parameters (index_path, scalers, channels, s3_*, etc.)\\n49\\t        **kwargs,\\n50\\t    ):\\n51\\t        if ds_match_direction not in [\\\"forward\\\", \\\"backward\\\", \\\"nearest\\\"]:\\n52\\t            raise ValueError(\\\"ds_match_direction must be one of 'forward', 'backward', or 'nearest'\\\")\\n53\\t\\n54\\t        # load_forecast_frames defaults to False here: flare forecasting supplies its\\n55\\t        # own labels, so future Surya frames never need to be fetched from disk/S3.\\n56\\t        kwargs.setdefault(\\\"load_forecast_frames\\\", False)\\n57\\t        super().__init__(**kwargs)\\n58\\t\\n59\\t        self.return_surya_stack = return_surya_stack\\n60\\t\\n61\\t        # Load ds index and find intersection with Surya index\\n62\\t        if filament_index_path is not None:\\n63\\t            self.ds_index = pd.read_csv(filament_index_path)\\n64\\t        else:\\n65\\t            raise ValueError(\\\"filament_index_path must be provided for FilamentDataset\\\")\\n66\\t\\n67\\t        # self.ds_index = pd.read_csv(ds_flare_index_path)\\n68\\t\\n69\\t        # self.ds_index[\\\"hemisphere\\\"] = self.ds_index[\\\"hemisphere\\\"].str.lower()\\n70\\t\\n71\\t        # self.ds_index[\\\"label\\\"] = self.ds_index[\\\"hemisphere\\\"].map({\\n72\\t        #     \\\"north\\\": 0,\\n73\\t        #     \\\"south\\\": 1,\\n74\\t        # })\\n75\\t\\n76\\t        if not self.ds_index[\\\"hemisphere\\\"].isin([0, 1]).all():\\n77\\t            raise ValueError(\\\"hemisphere column must contain only 0 or 1\\\")\\n78\\t        self.ds_index[\\\"label\\\"] = self.ds_index[\\\"hemisphere\\\"].astype(np.float32)\\n79\\t\\n80\\t        if self.ds_index[\\\"label\\\"].isna().any():\\n81\\t            raise ValueError(\\\"Hemisphere must be either 'North' or 'South'\\\")\\n82\\t\\n83\\t        self.ds_index[\\\"ds_index\\\"] = pd.to_datetime(\\n84\\t            self.ds_index[ds_time_column]\\n85\\t        ).values.astype(\\\"datetime64[ns]\\\")\\n86\\t        self.ds_index.sort_values(\\\"ds_index\\\", inplace=True)\\n87\\t\\n88\\t        # Create Surya valid indices and find closest match to DS index\\n89\\t        self.df_valid_indices = pd.DataFrame(\\n90\\t            {\\\"valid_indices\\\": self.valid_indices}\\n91\\t        ).sort_values(\\\"valid_indices\\\")\\n92\\t        self.df_valid_indices = pd.merge_asof(\\n93\\t            self.df_valid_indices,\\n94\\t            self.ds_index,\\n95\\t            right_on=\\\"ds_index\\\",\\n96\\t            left_on=\\\"valid_indices\\\",\\n97\\t            direction=ds_match_direction,\\n98\\t        )\\n99\\t        # Remove duplicates keeping closest match\\n100\\t        self.df_valid_indices[\\\"index_delta\\\"] = np.abs(\\n101\\t            self.df_valid_indices[\\\"valid_indices\\\"] - self.df_valid_indices[\\\"ds_index\\\"]\\n102\\t        )\\n103\\t        self.df_valid_indices = self.df_valid_indices.sort_values(\\n104\\t            [\\\"ds_index\\\", \\\"index_delta\\\"]\\n105\\t        )\\n106\\t        self.df_valid_indices.drop_duplicates(\\n107\\t            subset=\\\"ds_index\\\", keep=\\\"first\\\", inplace=True\\n108\\t        )\\n109\\t        # Enforce a maximum time tolerance for matches\\n110\\t        if ds_time_tolerance is not None:\\n111\\t            self.df_valid_indices = self.df_valid_indices.loc[\\n112\\t                self.df_valid_indices[\\\"index_delta\\\"] <= pd.Timedelta(ds_time_tolerance),\\n113\\t                :,\\n114\\t            ]\\n115\\t            if len(self.df_valid_indices) == 0:\\n116\\t                raise ValueError(\\\"No intersection between Surya and DS indices\\\")\\n117\\t\\n118\\t        # Override valid indices variables to reflect matches between Surya and DS\\n119\\t        self.valid_indices = [\\n120\\t            pd.Timestamp(date) for date in self.df_valid_indices[\\\"valid_indices\\\"]\\n121\\t        ]\\n122\\t        self.adjusted_length = len(self.valid_indices)\\n123\\t        self.df_valid_indices.set_index(\\\"valid_indices\\\", inplace=True)\\n124\\t\\n125\\t        if max_number_of_samples is not None and max_number_of_samples < self.adjusted_length:\\n126\\t            self.valid_indices = self.valid_indices[:max_number_of_samples]\\n127\\t            self.df_valid_indices = self.df_valid_indices.iloc[:max_number_of_samples]\\n128\\t            self.adjusted_length = max_number_of_samples\\n129\\t\\n130\\t    def __len__(self):\\n131\\t        return self.adjusted_length\\n132\\t\\n133\\t    def __getitem__(self, idx: int) -> dict:\\n134\\t        \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n135\\t        Args:\\n136\\t            idx: Dataset index.\\n137\\t\\n138\\t        Returns:\\n139\\t            Dictionary containing:\\n140\\t                forecast (np.float32): Normalized log10 flare intensity label.\\n141\\t                ds_index (str): ISO-format timestamp from the flare index.\\n142\\t            When ``return_surya_stack=True``, also includes all keys from\\n143\\t            ``HelioNetCDFDataset.__getitem__`` (ts, time_delta_input, lead_time_delta, etc.).\\n144\\t        \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n145\\t        sample = super().__getitem__(idx=idx) if self.return_surya_stack else {}\\n146\\t        sample[\\\"forecast\\\"] = np.float32(self.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx][\\\"label\\\"])\\n147\\t        sample[\\\"ds_index\\\"] = self.df_valid_indices[\\\"ds_index\\\"].iloc[idx].isoformat()\\n148\\t        return sample\\n149\\t\\n150\\t\\n151\\t\\n152\\t\",\n109\t            \"locations\": [\n110\t              \"/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/datasets/filament_dataset.py\"\n111\t            ]\n112\t          },\n113\t          {\n114\t            \"title\": \"Read ADAPTING.md\",\n115\t            \"status\": \"completed\",\n116\t            \"raw_input\": {\n117\t              \"file_path\": \"/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/ADAPTING.md\"\n118\t            },\n119\t            \"tool_call_id\": \"toolu_bdrk_016xyms85dymLUHnEJnFtaUE\",\n120\t            \"raw_output\": \"1\\t# Adapting the Template for Your Own Task\\n2\\t\\n3\\tThis guide walks you through copying the template fine-tuning app and wiring it to a new\\n4\\tdownstream task. Follow the steps in order \\u2014 each one builds on the previous.\\n5\\t\\n6\\tThe template task is **solar flare intensity regression** (predicting peak GOES X-ray flux\\n7\\tfrom SDO image stacks). The numbered scripts and notebooks in this folder give you working\\n8\\texamples of every step.\\n9\\t\\n10\\t**The one rule:** everything generic lives in `workshop_infrastructure/` and is *imported*,\\n11\\tnever copied. Your app owns only what is specific to your science. If you find yourself\\n12\\tcopying a file out of `workshop_infrastructure/`, stop \\u2014 that is the thing this layout\\n13\\texists to prevent.\\n14\\t\\n15\\t---\\n16\\t\\n17\\t## Overview: what the template gives you\\n18\\t\\n19\\t```\\n20\\tdownstream_apps/template/\\n21\\t\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 configs/config_script.yaml       \\u2190 single source of truth for all parameters\\n22\\t\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 configs.py                       \\u2190 FlareDataConfig: the ~4 task-specific config fields\\n23\\t\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb\\n24\\t\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 1_baseline_template.ipynb\\n25\\t\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 2_finetune_template_1D.ipynb\\n26\\t\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 3_finetune_template_1D.py        \\u2190 runnable training script (derived from notebook 2)\\n27\\t\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 ADAPTING.md                      \\u2190 this file\\n28\\t\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 datasets/\\n29\\t\\u2502   \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 template_dataset.py          \\u2190 FlareDSDataset (extends HelioNetCDFDataset)\\n30\\t\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 lightning_modules/\\n31\\t\\u2502   \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 pl_simple_baseline.py        \\u2190 FlareLightningModule (Lightning wrapper)\\n32\\t\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 metrics/\\n33\\t\\u2502   \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 template_metrics.py          \\u2190 FlareMetrics (loss + evaluation metrics)\\n34\\t\\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 models/\\n35\\t    \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 simple_baseline.py           \\u2190 RegressionFlareModel (linear baseline)\\n36\\t```\\n37\\t\\n38\\tAnd what it *imports* rather than owning:\\n39\\t\\n40\\t| From `workshop_infrastructure/` | What it gives you |\\n41\\t|---|---|\\n42\\t| `configs.py` | `DataConfig`, `TrainingConfig`, `ModelConfig`, `load_config()` \\u2014 the whole config layer |\\n43\\t| `datasets/helio.py` | `HelioNetCDFDataset` \\u2014 NetCDF loading, local + S3, normalization, frame sampling |\\n44\\t| `datasets/builders.py` | `build_helio_dataloaders()` \\u2014 maps your config onto ~20 dataset arguments |\\n45\\t| `models/finetune_models.py` | `HelioSpectformer1D` / `HelioSpectformer2D` \\u2014 backbone plus a configurable head |\\n46\\t| `utils.py` | `build_scalers()`, `apply_peft_lora()`, `load_pretrained_weights()`, S3 checkpoint upload |\\n47\\t\\n48\\t---\\n49\\t\\n50\\t## Step 1 \\u2014 Copy the template folder\\n51\\t\\n52\\t```bash\\n53\\tcp -r downstream_apps/template downstream_apps/your_task\\n54\\t```\\n55\\t\\n56\\tThen rename the classes that have \\\"Flare\\\" or \\\"Template\\\" in their names (Steps 2\\u20135 will\\n57\\ttell you exactly which ones to change).\\n58\\t\\n59\\t---\\n60\\t\\n61\\t## Step 2 \\u2014 Declare your task's config fields (`configs.py`)\\n62\\t\\n63\\t**File to edit:** `configs.py`.\\n64\\t\\n65\\tThis file is short by design. It subclasses the shared `DataConfig` with the handful of\\n66\\tfields your task needs, and binds `load_config` to that subclass:\\n67\\t\\n68\\t```python\\n69\\t@dataclass\\n70\\tclass YourDataConfig(DataConfig):\\n71\\t    your_catalog_path: str = \\\"\\\"\\n72\\t    your_label_column: str = \\\"flux\\\"\\n73\\t\\n74\\t    # Any field holding a filesystem path must join this list, so relative values in the\\n75\\t    # YAML resolve against the config file's directory instead of the working directory.\\n76\\t    PATH_FIELDS: ClassVar[tuple[str, ...]] = DataConfig.PATH_FIELDS + (\\\"your_catalog_path\\\",)\\n77\\t\\n78\\t\\n79\\tload_your_config = partial(load_config, data_cls=YourDataConfig)\\n80\\t```\\n81\\t\\n82\\tYou inherit every generic field \\u2014 paths, channels, temporal sampling, all the S3 settings,\\n83\\t`max_samples` \\u2014 without maintaining a copy of them.\\n84\\t\\n85\\tTwo behaviors worth knowing:\\n86\\t\\n87\\t- **Unknown keys are an error.** If the YAML has a key with no matching field, `load_config()`\\n88\\t  raises and lists the valid names. So add the field here *first*, then the YAML key.\\n89\\t- **Paths resolve relative to the config file**, and `~`/`$VARS` are expanded. Never commit an\\n90\\t  absolute path into someone else's home directory.\\n91\\t\\n92\\t---\\n93\\t\\n94\\t## Step 3 \\u2014 Define your dataset (`datasets/`)\\n95\\t\\n96\\t**File to edit:** `datasets/template_dataset.py` \\u2192 rename to `your_task_dataset.py`.\\n97\\t\\n98\\t`FlareDSDataset` extends `HelioNetCDFDataset` (in `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py`).\\n99\\tThe base class handles:\\n100\\t- Loading NetCDF files from local disk or S3\\n101\\t- Signum-log normalization per channel\\n102\\t- Input frame sampling and validity filtering\\n103\\t\\n104\\tYou only need to add your task-specific logic in the subclass:\\n105\\t\\n106\\t| What to override | Why |\\n107\\t|---|---|\\n108\\t| `__init__` | Accept your catalog/label source; pass everything else up via `super().__init__(**kwargs)` |\\n109\\t| `__getitem__` | Call `super().__getitem__()` to get the image stack, then attach your label |\\n110\\t\\n111\\t> **Normalization: know which space you are in.** The dataset applies signum-log\\n112\\t> compression *and then* a per-channel z-score, so \\\"undoing the transform\\\" is ambiguous.\\n113\\t> `scaler.inverse_transform()` undoes only the z-score, landing in **signum-log** space \\u2014\\n114\\t> that is what `destandardize_channels()` gives the linear baseline, and it is usually what\\n115\\t> you want as model input, since raw values span many orders of magnitude.\\n116\\t> `dataset.inverse_transform_data()` undoes **both** stages and returns true **physical**\\n117\\t> units (DN, Gauss), which is what you want for plotting or a physical-space loss.\\n118\\t> The full picture is in the \\\"THE THREE SPACES\\\" block in\\n119\\t> `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py`.\\n120\\t\\n121\\tIf your task supplies its own labels (rather than predicting future SDO frames), set\\n122\\t`kwargs.setdefault(\\\"load_forecast_frames\\\", False)` before calling `super().__init__()`, as\\n123\\t`FlareDSDataset` does. That stops the loader from fetching future frames it will never use \\u2014\\n124\\tworth roughly 1 GB of S3 traffic per sample.\\n125\\t\\n126\\t**Key YAML keys that feed into the dataset** (all under `data:`):\\n127\\t```yaml\\n128\\tdata:\\n129\\t  train_data_path: ...           # CSV index of NetCDF files (timestep, path, present)\\n130\\t  valid_data_path: ...\\n131\\t  channels: [...]                # Which SDO channels to load\\n132\\t  time_delta_input_minutes: [0]  # Temporal offsets for input frames\\n133\\t  time_delta_target_minutes: 60  # Step size between forecast frames\\n134\\t  s3_anon: true                  # true = public bucket; false = IAM credentials\\n135\\t  s3_mode: download              # download | simplecache | stream\\n136\\t  s3_cache_dir: ~/surya_ws_cache # Required unless s3_mode is \\\"stream\\\"\\n137\\t  max_samples: null              # Cap for quick experiments\\n138\\t```\\n139\\t\\n140\\t### Reading from S3\\n141\\t\\n142\\tThe pre-built indices in `data/indices/` point at `s3://nasa-surya-bench/...`, so `s3_mode`\\n143\\tand `s3_cache_dir` decide how your data actually arrives:\\n144\\t\\n145\\t| `s3_mode` | Behavior | Needs `s3_cache_dir`? |\\n146\\t|---|---|---|\\n147\\t| `download` (default) | Fetches each whole file into the cache with parallel multipart, then opens it locally. **Recommended** \\u2014 NetCDF/HDF5 needs random seeks that streaming cannot serve. | Yes |\\n148\\t| `simplecache` | fsspec read-through cache. | Yes |\\n149\\t| `stream` | Reads directly from S3, nothing written to disk. Works, but measured ~9x slower than `download` on a full SDO frame. Use only when disk space is the binding constraint. | No |\\n150\\t\\n151\\tThere is deliberately no default cache directory: each SDO file is ~1 GB, so the right\\n152\\tlocation depends on your machine. Budget ~1 GB \\u00d7 the number of unique timesteps. If your\\n153\\tindex has `s3://` paths and no cache directory is set, dataset construction fails\\n154\\timmediately with a suggested path \\u2014 not twenty minutes into training.\\n155\\t\\n156\\t`--s3-cache-dir` overrides it per machine without editing the committed config.\\n157\\t\\n158\\t---\\n159\\t\\n160\\t## Step 4 \\u2014 Define your metrics (`metrics/`)\\n161\\t\\n162\\t**File to edit:** `metrics/template_metrics.py` \\u2192 rename to `your_task_metrics.py`.\\n163\\t\\n164\\t`FlareMetrics` defines four metric sets selected by the `mode` argument at construction:\\n165\\t\\n166\\t| Mode | Purpose | Backpropagates? |\\n167\\t|---|---|---|\\n168\\t| `\\\"train_loss\\\"` | Loss that drives weight updates; logged as `train_loss` | Yes |\\n169\\t| `\\\"val_loss\\\"` | **Logged as `val_loss` \\u2014 the quantity `ModelCheckpoint` monitors** | No |\\n170\\t| `\\\"train_metrics\\\"` | Extra metrics logged during training | No |\\n171\\t| `\\\"val_metrics\\\"` | Metrics logged at validation, for reporting only | No |\\n172\\t\\n173\\tEach method returns `(dict[str, Tensor], list[float])`: a dict of named metric tensors\\n174\\tand a list of weights for combining multiple loss terms.\\n175\\t\\n176\\tThe dict keys become the metric names in WandB and CSV logs.\\n177\\t\\n178\\t> **Which metric selects checkpoints.** `val_loss` does \\u2014 not `val_metrics`. The names\\n179\\t> invite the opposite guess, so it is worth stating plainly: `val_metrics` is logged for\\n180\\t> reporting and has no effect on which checkpoint is kept.\\n181\\t>\\n182\\t> `FlareMetrics.val_loss` delegates to `train_loss` by default, so out of the box the\\n183\\t> monitored quantity has the same form as the training objective and the two cannot drift\\n184\\t> apart by accident. **Override `val_loss` in your metrics class** when your task needs a\\n185\\t> different validation objective \\u2014 that is the intended hook, and you should not need to\\n186\\t> touch `lightning_modules/`.\\n187\\t>\\n188\\t> The key is optional: `FlareLightningModule` falls back to `train_loss` if a metrics dict\\n189\\t> has no `\\\"val_loss\\\"` entry.\\n190\\t\\n191\\t---\\n192\\t\\n193\\t## Step 5 \\u2014 Define your model head (`models/`)\\n194\\t\\n195\\tFor 1D output tasks (regression, classification): use `HelioSpectformer1D` from\\n196\\t`workshop_infrastructure/models/finetune_models.py`. It wraps the Surya backbone with a\\n197\\tconfigurable pooling head and a linear output layer.\\n198\\t\\n199\\tFor 2D output tasks (pixel-level prediction): use `HelioSpectformer2D`.\\n200\\t\\n201\\tThe head is fully configured from the YAML `model:` section \\u2014 you usually don't need to\\n202\\ttouch the model code at all, just adjust the config:\\n203\\t\\n204\\t```yaml\\n205\\tmodel:\\n206\\t  pooling: class_token        # class_token | global_average | global_max | attention | transformer\\n207\\t  penultimate_linear_layer: true\\n208\\t  dropout: 0.2\\n209\\t  freeze_backbone: false\\n210\\t  use_lora: true\\n211\\t  lora_config:\\n212\\t    r: 8\\n213\\t    lora_alpha: 8\\n214\\t    ...\\n215\\t```\\n216\\t\\n217\\t`use_lora` and `freeze_backbone` together select the fine-tuning regime:\\n218\\t\\n219\\t| `use_lora` | `freeze_backbone` | Regime |\\n220\\t|---|---|---|\\n221\\t| `true` | \\u2014 | LoRA adapters on attention + FFN (default; small trainable count) |\\n222\\t| `false` | `true` | Linear probe \\u2014 only the head trains |\\n223\\t| `false` | `false` | Full fine-tuning of all 366M parameters |\\n224\\t\\n225\\tThe training script prints the trainable/total parameter count so you can confirm which\\n226\\tregime you actually got.\\n227\\t\\n228\\tIf your task needs a custom head (e.g. multi-head output, auxiliary losses), create a new\\n229\\tclass in `models/` following the `RegressionFlareModel` pattern in `simple_baseline.py`.\\n230\\t\\n231\\t---\\n232\\t\\n233\\t## Step 6 \\u2014 Wire it together in the training script\\n234\\t\\n235\\t**File to edit:** `3_finetune_template_1D.py`.\\n236\\t\\n237\\tOnly two of its four functions have task-specific content:\\n238\\t\\n239\\t| Function | What it does | What to change |\\n240\\t|---|---|---|\\n241\\t| `build_datasets` | Calls `build_helio_dataloaders()` | Swap `FlareDSDataset` for your subclass and replace the task-specific kwargs below it |\\n242\\t| `build_model` | Builds `HelioSpectformer1D`, loads weights, applies LoRA / freezing | Usually nothing \\u2014 driven by `cfg.model` |\\n243\\t| `build_trainer` | Loggers, checkpointing, Lightning Trainer | Nothing |\\n244\\t| `main` | Calls the above in order | Nothing |\\n245\\t\\n246\\t`build_datasets` should stay this short \\u2014 everything generic is already handled:\\n247\\t\\n248\\t```python\\n249\\tdef build_datasets(cfg):\\n250\\t    return build_helio_dataloaders(\\n251\\t        cfg,\\n252\\t        YourDataset,\\n253\\t        # \\u2193 only your task's kwargs belong here\\n254\\t        your_catalog_path=cfg.data.your_catalog_path,\\n255\\t        label_transform=_your_label_transform,\\n256\\t    )\\n257\\t```\\n258\\t\\n259\\tAlso change the `load_flare_config` import to your own `load_your_config`.\\n260\\t\\n261\\t---\\n262\\t\\n263\\t## Step 7 \\u2014 Edit `config_script.yaml`\\n264\\t\\n265\\tThis is the only file you need to edit between experiments. The sections map directly\\n266\\tto the dataclasses:\\n267\\t\\n268\\t| YAML section | Python dataclass | Accessed via |\\n269\\t|---|---|---|\\n270\\t| `data:` | your `DataConfig` subclass | `cfg.data.*` |\\n271\\t| `model:` | `ModelConfig` | `cfg.model.*` |\\n272\\t| `model.pretrained_path` | `ModelConfig.pretrained_path` | `cfg.model.pretrained_path` |\\n273\\t| `model.lora_config:` | `LoraAdapterConfig` | `cfg.model.lora_config.*` |\\n274\\t| `model.time_embedding:` | `TimeEmbeddingConfig` | `cfg.model.time_embedding.*` |\\n275\\t| `training:` | flat fields on `TrainingConfig` | `cfg.learning_rate`, `cfg.batch_size`, \\u2026 |\\n276\\t| `output:` | `OutputConfig` | `cfg.output.*` |\\n277\\t| `logging:` | flat fields on `TrainingConfig` | `cfg.wandb_project`, `cfg.wandb_entity` |\\n278\\t\\n279\\t---\\n280\\t\\n281\\t## Reproducibility\\n282\\t\\n283\\tTwo runs of the same config produce the same numbers. That is not free \\u2014 it is bought by two\\n284\\tkeys in the `training:` section, and it is worth understanding what they do before you change\\n285\\tthem.\\n286\\t\\n287\\t```yaml\\n288\\ttraining:\\n289\\t  seed: 42             # seeds Python, NumPy, torch, every DataLoader worker, and the shuffle\\n290\\t  deterministic: false # false | warn | true\\n291\\t```\\n292\\t\\n293\\t| `deterministic` | Behavior |\\n294\\t|---|---|\\n295\\t| `false` (default) | No guarantees. Two identical runs may disagree. Chosen as the default for throughput. |\\n296\\t| `warn` | Bit-identical wherever a deterministic CUDA kernel exists \\u2014 which is the whole model as shipped \\u2014 and a warning naming the op where one does not. Never blocks a run. |\\n297\\t| `true` | Hard guarantee: an op with no deterministic kernel raises. **Incompatible with `model.learned_flow: true`**, which uses `F.grid_sample` (no deterministic CUDA backward). |\\n298\\t\\n299\\tDeterminism costs roughly **+20% of wall time**: on this template (1 epoch, `max_samples: 10`,\\n300\\tone A100, LoRA) a run takes 159 s with `false` and 190 s with `warn`. That total includes a fixed\\n301\\t~1.8 GB checkpoint load, so the overhead on the compute alone is proportionally larger \\u2014 budget\\n302\\tmore than 20% on long runs. The default trades reproducibility for that throughput.\\n303\\t\\n304\\t**Switch to `warn` whenever you are comparing results.** With `false`, you cannot tell whether a\\n305\\tchange in your numbers came from your edit or from drift \\u2014 this config produced `val_loss`\\n306\\t0.060782 and 0.051657 on two runs that differed in nothing at all. Any before/after comparison,\\n307\\tablation, or hyperparameter sweep should use `warn`.\\n308\\t\\n309\\tFor a one-off comparison, pass the flag instead of editing the committed config:\\n310\\t\\n311\\t```bash\\n312\\tpython -m downstream_apps.your_task.3_finetune_template_1D --deterministic warn\\n313\\t```\\n314\\t\\n315\\tNote that `seed` still does its job with determinism off: the data order stays pinned, so a\\n316\\tdifferent seed still means a genuinely different run.\\n317\\t\\n318\\tTwo implementation details you inherit for free, but should not undo:\\n319\\t\\n320\\t- **`CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG` is set before `import torch`** \\u2014 at the top of\\n321\\t  `3_finetune_template_1D.py` and in the notebooks' first cell. cuBLAS reads it once at\\n322\\t  initialization, so setting it later silently does nothing. Without it you get a cuBLAS warning\\n323\\t  on every run.\\n324\\t- **The train DataLoader gets an explicit `generator` and `worker_init_fn`** (in\\n325\\t  `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py`). A bare `shuffle=True` seeds its sampler from\\n326\\t  whatever the global torch RNG state happens to be when the iterator is created \\u2014 so any code\\n327\\t  you add that consumes RNG beforehand would silently reshuffle your epochs. The worker seeder\\n328\\t  also seeds Python's `random`, because the channel masker in `helio.py` draws from it.\\n329\\t\\n330\\t> **Reproducibility is not significance.** With `max_samples: 10` and one epoch the result is\\n331\\t> still noise. This makes it the *same* noise every time, so that a change in the number can be\\n332\\t> attributed to your edit rather than to chance. Before drawing any scientific conclusion, raise\\n333\\t> `max_samples` and vary `seed`.\\n334\\t\\n335\\t---\\n336\\t\\n337\\t## Quick reference: running the script\\n338\\t\\n339\\t```bash\\n340\\t# Full run (--config defaults to this app's configs/config_script.yaml)\\n341\\tCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0,1 python -m downstream_apps.your_task.3_finetune_template_1D\\n342\\t\\n343\\t# Quick sanity check\\n344\\tCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python -m downstream_apps.your_task.3_finetune_template_1D \\\\\\n345\\t    --max-epochs 2 --no-wandb\\n346\\t\\n347\\t# Same config, different machine's scratch space\\n348\\tCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python -m downstream_apps.your_task.3_finetune_template_1D \\\\\\n349\\t    --s3-cache-dir /scratch/$USER/helio_cache\\n350\\t\\n351\\t# Reproducible run, for comparing a change against a baseline (~20% slower)\\n352\\tCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python -m downstream_apps.your_task.3_finetune_template_1D \\\\\\n353\\t    --deterministic warn\\n354\\t```\\n355\\t\\n356\\tSet `max_samples: 10` in the YAML while developing \\u2014 it limits the dataset so data loading\\n357\\tis fast without changing anything else.\\n358\\t\\n359\\t---\\n360\\t\\n361\\t## Checklist: files you should have edited\\n362\\t\\n363\\tIf you touched anything outside this list, ask whether it belongs in\\n364\\t`workshop_infrastructure/` instead:\\n365\\t\\n366\\t- [ ] `configs.py` \\u2014 your `DataConfig` subclass (~15 lines)\\n367\\t- [ ] `configs/config_script.yaml` \\u2014 your paths and hyperparameters\\n368\\t- [ ] `datasets/your_task_dataset.py` \\u2014 your labels\\n369\\t- [ ] `metrics/your_task_metrics.py` \\u2014 your loss and evaluation metrics\\n370\\t- [ ] `3_finetune_template_1D.py` \\u2014 the two imports and the kwargs in `build_datasets`\\n371\\t- [ ] `models/` \\u2014 only if you need a custom head\\n372\\t\",\n121\t            \"locations\": [\n122\t              \"/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/ADAPTING.md\"\n123\t            ],\n124\t            \"kind\": \"read\"\n125\t          },\n126\t          {\n127\t            \"status\": \"completed\",\n128\t            \"kind\": \"execute\",\n129\t            \"title\": \"echo \\\"--- checkpoint fil_data ---\\\"; cat data/.ipynb_checkpoints/fil_data-checkpoint.csv 2>/dev/null | head -50 echo \\\"--- diff between checkpoint and current ---\\\" diff data/.ipynb_checkpoints/fil_data-checkpoint.csv data/fil_data.csv echo \\\"--- any other csv/data files with chirality? ---\\\" grep -ril \\\"chiral\\\" filament_kyle 2>/dev/null grep -ril \\\"dextral\\\\|sinestral\\\\|sinistral\\\" filament_kyle 2>/dev/null\",\n130\t            \"tool_call_id\": \"toolu_bdrk_01SNyJGnTB1XDQwbtaNhHwA6\",\n131\t            \"raw_output\": \"--- checkpoint fil_data ---\\n\\ufeffstart_time,hemisphere,,,,,,\\r\\n2012-03-05T04:00:05,North,,,,,,\\r\\n2012-03-07T00:24:06,North,,,,,,\\r\\n2012-03-07T01:30:06,North,,,,,,\\r\\n2012-03-13T17:36:05,North,,,,,,\\r\\n2012-04-23T18:24:05,North,,,,,,\\r\\n2012-06-14T14:12:07,South,,,,,,\\r\\n2012-07-12T16:48:05,South,,,,,,\\r\\n2012-09-28T0:12:05,North,,,,,,\\r\\n2012-03-15T07:12:05,North,,,,,,\\r\\n2013-07-11T15:12:10,South,,,,,,\\r\\n2015-03-15T01:48:05,South,,,,,,\\r\\n,,,,,,,\\r\\n,,,,,,,\\r\\n,,,,,,,\\r\\n,,,,,,,--- diff between checkpoint and current ---\\n1,16c1,12\\n< \\ufeffstart_time,hemisphere,,,,,,\\r\\n< 2012-03-05T04:00:05,North,,,,,,\\r\\n< 2012-03-07T00:24:06,North,,,,,,\\r\\n< 2012-03-07T01:30:06,North,,,,,,\\r\\n< 2012-03-13T17:36:05,North,,,,,,\\r\\n< 2012-04-23T18:24:05,North,,,,,,\\r\\n< 2012-06-14T14:12:07,South,,,,,,\\r\\n< 2012-07-12T16:48:05,South,,,,,,\\r\\n< 2012-09-28T0:12:05,North,,,,,,\\r\\n< 2012-03-15T07:12:05,North,,,,,,\\r\\n< 2013-07-11T15:12:10,South,,,,,,\\r\\n< 2015-03-15T01:48:05,South,,,,,,\\r\\n< ,,,,,,,\\r\\n< ,,,,,,,\\r\\n< ,,,,,,,\\r\\n< ,,,,,,,\\n\\\\ No newline at end of file\\n---\\n> \\ufeffstart_time,hemisphere\\r\\n> 2012-03-05T04:00:05,1\\r\\n> 2012-03-07T00:24:06,1\\r\\n> 2012-03-07T01:30:06,1\\r\\n> 2012-03-13T17:36:05,1\\r\\n> 2012-04-23T18:24:05,1\\r\\n> 2012-06-14T14:12:07,0\\r\\n> 2012-07-12T16:48:05,0\\r\\n> 2012-09-28T00:12:05,1\\r\\n> 2012-03-15T07:12:05,1\\r\\n> 2013-07-11T15:12:10,0\\r\\n> 2015-03-15T01:48:05,0\\n\\\\ No newline at end of file\\n--- any other csv/data files with chirality? ---\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/untitled1.chat\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/brainstorming.chat\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/brainstorming.chat\\n/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/untitled1.chat\",\n132\t            \"raw_input\": {\n133\t              \"command\": \"echo \\\"--- checkpoint fil_data ---\\\"; cat data/.ipynb_checkpoints/fil_data-checkpoint.csv 2>/dev/null | head -50\\necho \\\"--- diff between checkpoint and current ---\\\"\\ndiff data/.ipynb_checkpoints/fil_data-checkpoint.csv data/fil_data.csv\\necho \\\"--- any other csv/data files with chirality? ---\\\"\\ngrep -ril \\\"chiral\\\" /home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle 2>/dev/null\\ngrep -ril \\\"dextral\\\\|sinestral\\\\|sinistral\\\" /home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle 2>/dev/null\"\n134\t            }\n135\t          },\n136\t          {\n137\t            \"kind\": \"execute\",\n138\t            \"raw_input\": {\n139\t              \"command\": \"wc -l brainstorming.chat untitled.chat untitled1.chat 2>/dev/null\\necho \\\"---\\\"\\ngrep -n -i \\\"chiral\\\\|dextral\\\\|sinestral\\\\|sinistral\\\\|45\\\" brainstorming.chat | head -60\"\n140\t            },\n141\t            \"title\": \"wc -l brainstorming.chat untitled.chat untitled1.chat 2>/dev/null echo \\\"---\\\" grep -n -i \\\"chiral\\\\|dextral\\\\|sinestral\\\\|sinistral\\\\|45\\\" brainstorming.chat | head -60\",\n142\t            \"tool_call_id\": \"toolu_bdrk_01DkvrqviEScd1BcTj5u2aKt\",\n143\t            \"status\": \"completed\",\n144\t            \"raw_output\": \"  394 brainstorming.chat\\n   56 untitled.chat\\n  221 untitled1.chat\\n  671 total\\n---\\n6:      \\\"body\\\": \\\"Hi, I would like to discuss my downstream application for filament classification. I want to start simple so that I can compare surya's results with a simple baseline. My idea is to use a catalog of 45 already classified filaments. So for example, could I try to use surya to classify if the filament is sinestral or dextral and compare against the \\\\\\\"ground truth\\\\\\\" of the catalog.\\\",\\n65:      \\\"body\\\": \\\"Yes, that's a reasonable first experiment \\\\u2014 binary sinistral/dextral is a clean, well-defined task, and `finetune_models.py` already has classification-head support to build on. The real constraint is N=45: that's too small for a conventional train/val/test split, so I'd lean toward a frozen-backbone linear probe (not LoRA/full fine-tune) plus leave-one-out or k-fold cross-validation to get any usable signal, and compare against a trivial baseline (e.g. majority-class or a simple hand-engineered feature) rather than a trained baseline model that would also starve on 45 samples.\\\\n\\\\nWant me to sketch out how this would map onto the `downstream_apps/filament_kyle` template (config, dataset loader for the catalog, classification head/metrics)?\\\",\\n83:      \\\"body\\\": \\\"is N=45 okay for a toy example? I'm not ready to scale up to a large amount of images. I'm just trying to understand how to make a downstream application for surya\\\",\\n95:      \\\"body\\\": \\\"45 is fine for that purpose \\\\u2014 you're not trying to get a publishable model, you're trying to learn the mechanics of wiring a downstream app to Surya (dataset loader, config, head, metrics, LoRA vs. probe knobs) end to end. Just treat any accuracy number as illustrative, not evidence Surya \\\\\\\"works\\\\\\\" for chirality, since with 45 samples the result could flip with a different random split.\\\\n\\\\nWant me to sketch how this maps onto the `filament_kyle` template (catalog \\\\u2192 CSV index, dataset class, classification head/metrics, config)?\\\"\\n111:      \\\"body\\\": \\\"Instead of chirality, could I also classify by hemisphere or quadrant or another solar surface region classification?\\\"\\n116:      \\\"body\\\": \\\"Yes, that works mechanically the same way \\\\u2014 same catalog-to-CSV, dataset, and classification-head pattern, just swap the label column. The tradeoff: hemisphere/quadrant is largely a *positional* attribute, so if your input crop retains any spatial context, a trivial baseline (or even just image coordinates) could nail it near-perfectly, making it a good pipeline sanity check but a weak test of whether Surya's embeddings capture anything non-trivial \\\\u2014 chirality is the more interesting comparison because it's a physical property, not just \\\\\\\"where is this pixel.\\\\\\\"\\\",\\n134:      \\\"body\\\": \\\"So with classifying chirality, what extra steps do I need to do to make sure I'm showing Surya just the filament, not just the entire disk of the Sun?\\\",\\n142:      \\\"id\\\": \\\"3e053ae6-4969-4590-a44b-1376c1da2ef2\\\",\\n177:            \\\"raw_output\\\": \\\"1\\\\timport numpy as np\\\\n2\\\\timport pandas as pd\\\\n3\\\\tfrom typing import Callable, Literal\\\\n4\\\\tfrom workshop_infrastructure.datasets.helio import HelioNetCDFDataset\\\\n5\\\\t\\\\n6\\\\t\\\\n7\\\\tclass FilamentDataset(HelioNetCDFDataset):\\\\n8\\\\t    \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n9\\\\t    Template child class of HelioNetCDFDataset showing how to build a downstream dataset.\\\\n10\\\\t    Extends the base class with a flare intensity label aligned to the Surya index.\\\\n11\\\\t\\\\n12\\\\t    All ``HelioNetCDFDataset`` keyword arguments (``index_path``, ``scalers``, ``channels``,\\\\n13\\\\t    ``s3_cache_dir``, etc.) are accepted via ``**kwargs`` and forwarded to the base class.\\\\n14\\\\t    ``load_forecast_frames`` defaults to ``False`` here (flare forecasting supplies its own\\\\n15\\\\t    labels, so future Surya frames are never fetched); pass it explicitly to override.\\\\n16\\\\t\\\\n17\\\\t    Additional Args:\\\\n18\\\\t        return_surya_stack: If True (default), include the Surya image stack in the returned dict.\\\\n19\\\\t            Set to False to return only the flare intensity label (useful for label inspection).\\\\n20\\\\t        max_number_of_samples: Cap the dataset length at this value. Useful for quick experiments.\\\\n21\\\\t        label_transform: Optional callable applied to the ``intensity`` column of the flare index\\\\n22\\\\t            to produce the ``normalized_intensity`` label. Signature:\\\\n23\\\\t            ``(series: pd.Series) -> pd.Series``.  If ``None``, the raw intensity values are\\\\n24\\\\t            used as-is. Define this at the call site (e.g., in ``build_datasets()``) to keep\\\\n25\\\\t            normalization logic out of the dataset class.\\\\n26\\\\t        flare_index_path: Path to the downstream flare intensity CSV index.\\\\n27\\\\t        ds_time_column: Column name in the flare index to use as the event timestamp.\\\\n28\\\\t        ds_time_tolerance: Maximum allowed time offset when matching Surya and DS indices\\\\n29\\\\t            (e.g., ``\\\\\\\"15min\\\\\\\"``). Unmatched entries are dropped.\\\\n30\\\\t        ds_match_direction: Merge direction passed to ``pd.merge_asof``. Use ``\\\\\\\"forward\\\\\\\"``\\\\n31\\\\t            for causal prediction (predict flares from prior solar state).\\\\n32\\\\t\\\\n33\\\\t    Raises:\\\\n34\\\\t        ValueError: If ``filament_index_path`` is not provided, or if no overlap exists\\\\n35\\\\t            between the Surya and DS indices within the specified tolerance.\\\\n36\\\\t    \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n37\\\\t\\\\n38\\\\t    def __init__(\\\\n39\\\\t        self,\\\\n40\\\\t        # Downstream-specific parameters\\\\n41\\\\t        return_surya_stack: bool = True,\\\\n42\\\\t        max_number_of_samples: int | None = None,\\\\n43\\\\t        label_transform: Callable[[pd.Series], pd.Series] | None = None,\\\\n44\\\\t        filament_index_path: str | None = None,\\\\n45\\\\t        ds_time_column: str | None = None,\\\\n46\\\\t        ds_time_tolerance: str | None = None,\\\\n47\\\\t        ds_match_direction: Literal[\\\\\\\"forward\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"backward\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"nearest\\\\\\\"] = \\\\\\\"forward\\\\\\\",\\\\n48\\\\t        # All HelioNetCDFDataset parameters (index_path, scalers, channels, s3_*, etc.)\\\\n49\\\\t        **kwargs,\\\\n50\\\\t    ):\\\\n51\\\\t        if ds_match_direction not in [\\\\\\\"forward\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"backward\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"nearest\\\\\\\"]:\\\\n52\\\\t            raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"ds_match_direction must be one of 'forward', 'backward', or 'nearest'\\\\\\\")\\\\n53\\\\t\\\\n54\\\\t        # load_forecast_frames defaults to False here: flare forecasting supplies its\\\\n55\\\\t        # own labels, so future Surya frames never need to be fetched from disk/S3.\\\\n56\\\\t        kwargs.setdefault(\\\\\\\"load_forecast_frames\\\\\\\", False)\\\\n57\\\\t        super().__init__(**kwargs)\\\\n58\\\\t\\\\n59\\\\t        self.return_surya_stack = return_surya_stack\\\\n60\\\\t\\\\n61\\\\t        # Load ds index and find intersection with Surya index\\\\n62\\\\t        if filament_index_path is not None:\\\\n63\\\\t            self.ds_index = pd.read_csv(filament_index_path)\\\\n64\\\\t        else:\\\\n65\\\\t            raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"filament_index_path must be provided for FilamentDataset\\\\\\\")\\\\n66\\\\t\\\\n67\\\\t        # self.ds_index = pd.read_csv(ds_flare_index_path)\\\\n68\\\\t\\\\n69\\\\t        # self.ds_index[\\\\\\\"hemisphere\\\\\\\"] = self.ds_index[\\\\\\\"hemisphere\\\\\\\"].str.lower()\\\\n70\\\\t\\\\n71\\\\t        # self.ds_index[\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\"] = self.ds_index[\\\\\\\"hemisphere\\\\\\\"].map({\\\\n72\\\\t        #     \\\\\\\"north\\\\\\\": 0,\\\\n73\\\\t        #     \\\\\\\"south\\\\\\\": 1,\\\\n74\\\\t        # })\\\\n75\\\\t\\\\n76\\\\t        if not self.ds_index[\\\\\\\"hemisphere\\\\\\\"].isin([0, 1]).all():\\\\n77\\\\t            raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"hemisphere column must contain only 0 or 1\\\\\\\")\\\\n78\\\\t        self.ds_index[\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\"] = self.ds_index[\\\\\\\"hemisphere\\\\\\\"].astype(np.float32)\\\\n79\\\\t\\\\n80\\\\t        if self.ds_index[\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\"].isna().any():\\\\n81\\\\t            raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"Hemisphere must be either 'North' or 'South'\\\\\\\")\\\\n82\\\\t\\\\n83\\\\t        self.ds_index[\\\\\\\"ds_index\\\\\\\"] = pd.to_datetime(\\\\n84\\\\t            self.ds_index[ds_time_column]\\\\n85\\\\t        ).values.astype(\\\\\\\"datetime64[ns]\\\\\\\")\\\\n86\\\\t        self.ds_index.sort_values(\\\\\\\"ds_index\\\\\\\", inplace=True)\\\\n87\\\\t\\\\n88\\\\t        # Create Surya valid indices and find closest match to DS index\\\\n89\\\\t        self.df_valid_indices = pd.DataFrame(\\\\n90\\\\t            {\\\\\\\"valid_indices\\\\\\\": self.valid_indices}\\\\n91\\\\t        ).sort_values(\\\\\\\"valid_indices\\\\\\\")\\\\n92\\\\t        self.df_valid_indices = pd.merge_asof(\\\\n93\\\\t            self.df_valid_indices,\\\\n94\\\\t            self.ds_index,\\\\n95\\\\t            right_on=\\\\\\\"ds_index\\\\\\\",\\\\n96\\\\t            left_on=\\\\\\\"valid_indices\\\\\\\",\\\\n97\\\\t            direction=ds_match_direction,\\\\n98\\\\t        )\\\\n99\\\\t        # Remove duplicates keeping closest match\\\\n100\\\\t        self.df_valid_indices[\\\\\\\"index_delta\\\\\\\"] = np.abs(\\\\n101\\\\t            self.df_valid_indices[\\\\\\\"valid_indices\\\\\\\"] - self.df_valid_indices[\\\\\\\"ds_index\\\\\\\"]\\\\n102\\\\t        )\\\\n103\\\\t        self.df_valid_indices = self.df_valid_indices.sort_values(\\\\n104\\\\t            [\\\\\\\"ds_index\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"index_delta\\\\\\\"]\\\\n105\\\\t        )\\\\n106\\\\t        self.df_valid_indices.drop_duplicates(\\\\n107\\\\t            subset=\\\\\\\"ds_index\\\\\\\", keep=\\\\\\\"first\\\\\\\", inplace=True\\\\n108\\\\t        )\\\\n109\\\\t        # Enforce a maximum time tolerance for matches\\\\n110\\\\t        if ds_time_tolerance is not None:\\\\n111\\\\t            self.df_valid_indices = self.df_valid_indices.loc[\\\\n112\\\\t                self.df_valid_indices[\\\\\\\"index_delta\\\\\\\"] <= pd.Timedelta(ds_time_tolerance),\\\\n113\\\\t                :,\\\\n114\\\\t            ]\\\\n115\\\\t            if len(self.df_valid_indices) == 0:\\\\n116\\\\t                raise ValueError(\\\\\\\"No intersection between Surya and DS indices\\\\\\\")\\\\n117\\\\t\\\\n118\\\\t        # Override valid indices variables to reflect matches between Surya and DS\\\\n119\\\\t        self.valid_indices = [\\\\n120\\\\t            pd.Timestamp(date) for date in self.df_valid_indices[\\\\\\\"valid_indices\\\\\\\"]\\\\n121\\\\t        ]\\\\n122\\\\t        self.adjusted_length = len(self.valid_indices)\\\\n123\\\\t        self.df_valid_indices.set_index(\\\\\\\"valid_indices\\\\\\\", inplace=True)\\\\n124\\\\t\\\\n125\\\\t        if max_number_of_samples is not None and max_number_of_samples < self.adjusted_length:\\\\n126\\\\t            self.valid_indices = self.valid_indices[:max_number_of_samples]\\\\n127\\\\t            self.df_valid_indices = self.df_valid_indices.iloc[:max_number_of_samples]\\\\n128\\\\t            self.adjusted_length = max_number_of_samples\\\\n129\\\\t\\\\n130\\\\t    def __len__(self):\\\\n131\\\\t        return self.adjusted_length\\\\n132\\\\t\\\\n133\\\\t    def __getitem__(self, idx: int) -> dict:\\\\n134\\\\t        \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n135\\\\t        Args:\\\\n136\\\\t            idx: Dataset index.\\\\n137\\\\t\\\\n138\\\\t        Returns:\\\\n139\\\\t            Dictionary containing:\\\\n140\\\\t                forecast (np.float32): Normalized log10 flare intensity label.\\\\n141\\\\t                ds_index (str): ISO-format timestamp from the flare index.\\\\n142\\\\t            When ``return_surya_stack=True``, also includes all keys from\\\\n143\\\\t            ``HelioNetCDFDataset.__getitem__`` (ts, time_delta_input, lead_time_delta, etc.).\\\\n144\\\\t        \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n145\\\\t        sample = super().__getitem__(idx=idx) if self.return_surya_stack else {}\\\\n146\\\\t        sample[\\\\\\\"forecast\\\\\\\"] = np.float32(self.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx][\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\"])\\\\n147\\\\t        sample[\\\\\\\"ds_index\\\\\\\"] = self.df_valid_indices[\\\\\\\"ds_index\\\\\\\"].iloc[idx].isoformat()\\\\n148\\\\t        return sample\\\\n149\\\\t\\\\n150\\\\t\\\\n151\\\\t\\\\n152\\\\t\\\",\\n200:      \\\"body\\\": \\\"No built-in cropping/ROI support in the base dataset \\\\u2014 it hands you the full 4096\\\\u00d74096 disk stack. So to show Surya \\\\\\\"just the filament\\\\\\\" rather than the whole disk, you'd need to add:\\\\n\\\\n1. **Location metadata in the catalog** \\\\u2014 your `fil_data.csv` currently only has `start_time, hemisphere`, no filament coordinates. You need per-filament position (heliographic lat/lon, or pixel/helioprojective bounding box + length/position angle \\\\u2014 typical of filament catalogs) to know *where* to crop.\\\\n2. **A coordinate-to-pixel mapping** \\\\u2014 convert catalog coordinates into pixel indices on the 4096\\\\u00d74096 frame (accounting for the observation's WCS/roll at that timestamp \\\\u2014 solar rotation means the same heliographic location moves in pixel space over time).\\\\n3. **A crop step in `FilamentDataset.__getitem__`** \\\\u2014 after `super().__getitem__()` returns the full stack, slice out a window around that pixel location, sized as a multiple of patch size 16 (e.g., 128\\\\u00d7128 or 256\\\\u00d7256) with some padding.\\\\n4. **A decision on channels** \\\\u2014 chirality is read off the polarity-inversion-line/magnetic structure, so HMI channels matter most; AIA 304/171 show the filament as a dark feature but may add background clutter you don't want the model keying on.\\\\n\\\\nThe bigger tradeoff to flag: with only 45 samples, hand-cropping introduces a new failure mode \\\\u2014 if your crop is centered too precisely on catalog coordinates, you're leaking the \\\\\\\"correct\\\\\\\" answer's spatial context, but too loose a crop and you're back to showing background disk features. Want me to look at what columns are actually available in the real 45-filament catalog you plan to use, so I can tell you concretely what crop logic is feasible?\\\",\\n219:      \\\"id\\\": \\\"952f9298-e9a6-4d15-806e-df345b2d97f8\\\",\\n224:      \\\"body\\\": \\\"That simplifies things \\\\u2014 no coordinate mapping or crop logic needed, and it matches what `FilamentDataset` already does (matches by timestamp only, no location). The tradeoff to be aware of: at full-disk resolution the model has to implicitly find the right filament among everything else in the frame, so if the catalog timestamp coincides with other active regions/filaments, the label is ambiguous about which feature it refers to \\\\u2014 with 45 samples, that ambiguity is a bigger risk to a clean signal than the localization work would have been.\\\",\\n297:      \\\"body\\\": \\\"Mechanically yes \\\\u2014 but it's not plumbed in today: the classification head takes only the pooled Surya embedding, so you'd need to modify it to concatenate normalized lat/lon onto that embedding before the final linear layer, similar in spirit to how the backbone already gets time metadata, but applied at the head instead.\\\\n\\\\nThe bigger issue is scientific, not architectural: filament chirality is known to follow a hemispheric pattern (Martin's rule) \\\\u2014 northern-hemisphere filaments are predominantly dextral, southern predominantly sinistral, correct ~90% of the time just from sign of latitude alone. So handing the model latitude risks letting it (or your \\\\\\\"baseline\\\\\\\") solve the task via that shortcut rather than the actual image content, which would undercut the comparison you're trying to make between Surya and a naive baseline.\\\",\\n319:      \\\"body\\\": \\\"That's a solid rationale \\\\u2014 using a known, strong pattern as ground truth means you have a clear signal to sanity-check the whole pipeline (data loading, head architecture, training loop) against, rather than hoping a weak/noisy signal shows up in 45 samples. The \\\\\\\"shortcut\\\\\\\" concern I raised isn't a problem here since it's literally the thing you're testing for.\\\\n\\\\nThis will touch a few pieces: adding lat/lon (or hemisphere) columns to your catalog, updating `FilamentDataset` to carry them through, and modifying the classification head to concatenate them with the pooled embedding. Want me to put together an implementation plan for that?\\\",\\n320:      \\\"id\\\": \\\"0376ce2c-f153-4527-b8c2-5146c36faabf\\\",\"\n145\t          },\n146\t          {\n147\t            \"status\": \"completed\",\n148\t            \"raw_input\": {\n149\t              \"limit\": 75.0,\n150\t              \"offset\": 320.0,\n151\t              \"file_path\": \"/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/filament_kyle/brainstorming.chat\"\n152\t            },\n153\t            \"raw_output\": \"320\\t      \\\"id\\\": \\\"0376ce2c-f153-4527-b8c2-5146c36faabf\\\",\\n321\\t      \\\"sender\\\": \\\"jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona\\\",\\n322\\t      \\\"type\\\": \\\"msg\\\",\\n323\\t      \\\"mentions\\\": []\\n324\\t    }\\n325\\t  ],\\n326\\t  \\\"users\\\": {\\n327\\t    \\\"jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::CopilotAcpPersona\\\": {\\n328\\t      \\\"username\\\": \\\"jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::CopilotAcpPersona\\\",\\n329\\t      \\\"color\\\": null,\\n330\\t      \\\"avatar_url\\\": \\\"/user/kwebster/api/ai/avatars/jupyter-ai-personas%3A%3Ajupyter_ai_acp_client%3A%3ACopilotAcpPersona\\\",\\n331\\t      \\\"bot\\\": true,\\n332\\t      \\\"display_name\\\": \\\"Copilot\\\",\\n333\\t      \\\"initials\\\": null,\\n334\\t      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Copilot\\\"\\n335\\t    },\\n336\\t    \\\"kwebster\\\": {\\n337\\t      \\\"color\\\": \\\"var(--jp-collaborator-color7)\\\",\\n338\\t      \\\"u