      "body": "Here's what needs to change now that `chirality` (dextral=0, sinistral=1) is a real column in `basic_fil_data.csv`, and why:\n\n**1. `datasets/filament_dataset.py` \u2014 the label source itself.**\nRight now the target label comes from `hemisphere`:\n```python\nif not self.ds_index[\"hemisphere\"].isin([0, 1]).all():\n    raise ValueError(\"hemisphere column must contain only 0 or 1\")\nself.ds_index[\"label\"] = self.ds_index[\"hemisphere\"].astype(np.float32)\n```\nThat's backwards for your actual task now \u2014 `chirality` is what Surya should be predicting; `hemisphere` is the input the *baseline* (Martin's Rule) uses to guess at chirality. So this needs to become:\n```python\nif not self.ds_index[\"chirality\"].isin([0, 1]).all():\n    raise ValueError(\"chirality column must contain only 0 (dextral) or 1 (sinistral)\")\nself.ds_index[\"label\"] = self.ds_index[\"chirality\"].astype(np.float32)\n```\n\n**2. Keep `hemisphere` around, but only as metadata \u2014 not as model input.**\nYou still need `hemisphere` per-event to compute the Martin's Rule baseline and to check whether Surya is doing better than \"just look at hemisphere.\" Since `merge_asof` already carries every catalog column through into `self.df_valid_indices`, the cheapest fix is exposing it in `__getitem__` alongside `forecast`:\n```python\nsample[\"hemisphere\"] = int(self.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx][\"hemisphere\"])\n```\nThe important constraint from your earlier reasoning still applies: this should never get concatenated into `batch[\"ts\"]` or otherwise handed to the model as a feature \u2014 that's the exact shortcut you're testing against, so it stays passthrough-only, for reporting.\n\n**3. `configs/config_script.yaml` \u2014 the path.**\n`filament_index_path` currently points at `../data/fil_data.csv`. You'll need to point it at `../data/basic_fil_data.csv` (or decide `basic_fil_data.csv` is now the permanent name and delete the old stub \u2014 up to you, since you said the 45-row version is still coming).\n\n**4. Worth flagging, not blocking:** the target key in every batch dict is still named `\"forecast\"` \u2014 a leftover from the flare-intensity template. It's functionally fine (nothing about the name enforces regression vs. classification), but it'll read oddly once you're doing chirality classification. I'd rename it to something like `\"label\"` when we touch the metrics/lightning module for the classification baseline, rather than now.\n\nWant me to make edits 1\u20133 now? And should I treat `basic_fil_data.csv` as the permanent filename going forward, or will the real 45-row file replace it under the original `fil_data.csv` name later?",
