    {
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789502306.678824,
      "body": "I want the baseline to be a coin-flip to start. I know it's not very impressive, but I specifically have events that might be exceptions to Martin's Rule, so it may already suffer in comparison from that higher percentage baseline and I'll compare with coin-flip instead. But don't make the changes yet, I want an explanation of the coin-flip baseline development.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "kwebster",
      "metadata": {
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
        "settings": {},
        "model": {
          "settings": {},
          "id": "sonnet"
        }
      },
      "id": "0dba0c92-9a39-4b46-af7a-b48681ce4ad2"
    },
    {
      "time": 1789502322.4367964,
      "body": "Here's the plan for the coin-flip baseline, before I touch anything.\n\n**Why coin-flip instead of Martin's Rule:** Martin's Rule is only a good baseline if your catalog is \"typical.\" Since you specifically curated events that may violate it, using it as the baseline would either flatter Surya (beating a baseline that's handicapped on your data) or unfairly bury it (if the exceptions are hard cases for everyone). A 50/50 coin-flip has no assumptions about the data \u2014 it's a neutral floor. Nothing stops you from also reporting Martin's Rule later for context; it just won't be *the* baseline.\n\n**What \"coin-flip baseline\" concretely means here:**\n- It doesn't touch hemisphere, images, or any feature \u2014 it's pure chance, so there's nothing to leak and nothing to train.\n- Because N is tiny (11 now, 45 eventually), a *single* random draw of guesses is a noisy, arbitrary number \u2014 you could luck into 70% accuracy by chance alone. So instead of one draw, I'd run a Monte Carlo simulation: thousands of trials of \"guess randomly for all N events,\" compute accuracy each trial, then report the distribution (mean \u2248 0.5, plus spread/percentiles). That distribution is the actual thing to compare Surya against \u2014 if Surya's accuracy falls inside the range chance alone produces at this N, it hasn't demonstrated real signal yet.\n\n**Data plumbing:**\n- I'd build `FilamentDataset` with `return_surya_stack=False` (from notebook 0's pattern) so no images are downloaded \u2014 the coin flip only needs the `chirality` labels, not pixels.\n- I'd still restrict to events that have a valid match against the Surya index (same matching logic that'll apply when you eventually run the real model), so the baseline is scored on the same event population the model will be scored on \u2014 not the full raw catalog. I'd combine matches from both the train and val Surya indices (dedup by timestamp) since a non-trained baseline has no real notion of a train/val split.\n- I'd drop the `max_number_of_samples=6` cap from notebook 0 (that was just for quick exploration) so the baseline uses every matched event.\n- `hemisphere` stays loaded as passthrough metadata (per the earlier edits) but is not referenced anywhere in the coin-flip computation \u2014 kept only so a future Martin's-Rule comparison can reuse the same dataset object without re-plumbing.\n\n**What gets removed from the current notebook:** the whole PyTorch Lightning / linear-regression / WandB apparatus (model, metrics classes, trainer, logger) \u2014 none of that applies to a coin flip. The rewritten notebook would be much shorter: config \u2192 dataset \u2192 labels \u2192 Monte Carlo accuracy distribution \u2192 a couple of printed summary numbers.\n\nLet me know if that matches what you had in mind, and I'll make the edits.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "19a40f00-6470-43a8-b5ab-3fd20d8b04a2",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": []
    },
