      "body": "No more digging \u2014 here's what's happening, based on what I checked above:\n\n**Where it's failing:** `build_helio_dataloaders` always builds *two* datasets \u2014 one against `cfg.data.train_data_path`, one against `cfg.data.valid_data_path`. Your traceback shows it dying on the second one, `val_dataset`, inside `FilamentDataset.__init__`, at the line that enforces `ds_time_tolerance`. That means: of your 11 catalog events, zero landed within 2 days of any timestamp in the **val** Surya index.\n\n**Why val specifically has nothing:** I checked `data/indices/surya_aws_s3_val.csv` \u2014 it isn't a random sample across all years, it's a narrow held-out slice: **Jan 16\u201330 of each year, only**. Your 11 filament events are dated March, April, June, July, September, and November \u2014 none of them are in January. So val matching zero events isn't a bug in your code, it's just that your catalog's dates don't fall inside that held-out window at all.\n\n**Separately, worth knowing before you rely on train either:** I also checked `surya_aws_s3_train.csv`, and it's missing 2012 entirely (jumps 2011 \u2192 2013). 9 of your 11 events are in 2012. So even once val stops erroring, matching against train alone will only pick up 2 of your 11 events (the 2013-11-07 and 2015-03-15 ones) \u2014 the rest have no corresponding Surya timestep in *either* split, at least at the paths this config points to.\n\nSo there are two separate issues bundled into one error:\n1. **Immediate crash cause:** `build_helio_dataloaders` requires both a train and val match; val has none for your dates, so it raises.\n2. **Underlying data gap:** even fixing #1, only 2/11 events would actually have any Surya frame to align to, because 2012 isn't in the indices this config uses.\n\nGiven that, do you want to keep using `build_helio_dataloaders` at all for this notebook? A coin-flip baseline doesn't need a train/val split \u2014 it could just build one `FilamentDataset` directly (like notebook 0 does) and skip this crash entirely. But that still leaves problem #2: most of your 2012 events wouldn't have a matched Surya frame under the current indices, so the coin-flip baseline's event count could shrink from 11 to 2 unless you point at a different/fuller index or investigate why 2012 is absent.",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
    },
