    {
      "time": 1789566102.2725523,
      "type": "msg",
      "mentions": [],
      "body": "Yes \u2014 2012 data exists in the S3 bucket itself; it's just missing from the local index CSVs (`surya_aws_s3_train/val/test.csv`). I checked `s3://nasa-surya-bench` directly (anonymous access):\n\n```\n2012/01/... 2012/03/... 2012/04/... 2012/06/... 2012/07/... 2012/09/...\n```\n\nAll present, at the normal 12-min cadence (e.g. `2012/03/20120301_0000.nc`, `2012/03/20120301_0012.nc`, ...). So the gap is specifically an **index-file** gap, not a data-availability gap \u2014 whoever built those three CSVs excluded 2012 (and 2022) from the train/val/test split, but the underlying NetCDF files were never deleted from the bucket.\n\nThat means your 9 missing-in-2012 events aren't actually unreachable \u2014 you'd just need an index entry pointing at the right `s3://nasa-surya-bench/2012/...` path for each date, rather than relying on `surya_aws_s3_train.csv`/`_val.csv`. Want me to check whether files exist at the exact timestamps near your 9 events (not just month-level), so we know precisely what a supplemental 2012 index would need?",
      "id": "f729a032-681c-42d5-a40d-d882edc8c8cc",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
