      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Good news \u2014 all 9 of your 2012 events have a matching file in S3 within minutes (well inside the 2-day tolerance), so this is very doable. Concretely:\n\n| event | nearest available S3 frame |\n|---|---|\n| 2012-03-05 04:00:05 | 2012-03-05 04:12:00 |\n| 2012-03-07 00:24:06 | 2012-03-07 00:36:00 |\n| 2012-03-07 01:30:06 | 2012-03-07 01:36:00 |\n| 2012-03-13 17:36:05 | 2012-03-13 17:48:00 |\n| 2012-03-15 07:12:05 | 2012-03-15 08:12:00 |\n| 2012-04-23 18:24:05 | 2012-04-23 18:36:00 |\n| 2012-06-14 14:12:07 | 2012-06-14 14:24:00 |\n| 2012-07-12 16:48:05 | 2012-07-12 17:00:00 |\n| 2012-09-28 00:12:05 | 2012-09-28 00:24:00 |\n\n**Why this is straightforward:** I checked `_filter_valid_indices()` in `helio.py` \u2014 since your config has `time_delta_input_minutes: [0]` and `FilamentDataset` sets `load_forecast_frames=False`, a Surya index row is \"valid\" as long as that exact timestep is present. No surrounding window of frames is required. So all I need is one CSV row per event: `path, timestep, present, dayofyear`, pointing at the S3 key I already confirmed exists.\n\n**Design choice to flag before I build anything:** rather than editing the shared 467K-row `data/indices/surya_aws_s3_train.csv` (used by other downstream apps too), I'd rather build a small, app-local index CSV under `downstream_apps/filament_kyle/data/` containing just the Surya timesteps your 11 catalog events actually need \u2014 the 2 that already matched (2013-11-07, 2015-03-15) plus these 9 new 2012 ones, all sourced the same way (confirmed S3 listing). Then point `train_data_path` in this app's `config_script.yaml` at that new file instead of the shared one. That keeps the fix scoped to this app, keeps the file small/readable, and doesn't risk affecting any other downstream app that reads the shared index.\n\nWant me to go ahead and build that index file + update the config this way?"
    },
