      "id": "3a39076e-dfa4-4911-90c8-0ec0d0609397",
      "body": "All 11 events now match correctly \u2014 every `ds_index` timestamp lines up exactly with its own catalog row (compare against `basic_fil_data.csv`: labels and hemispheres both match 1:1). Both `train` and `val` datasets report 11 samples, since they're currently pointed at the same file.\n\nThat confirms the CSV is now correct and `build_helio_dataloaders` works end-to-end with it. Nothing was written to `config_script.yaml` \u2014 this was all done with an in-memory `cfg` override, same as before.Yes \u2014 functionally, what I just ran *is* the \"build the helio dataloader\" step; the current notebook's version of that cell (`70e9c5b2`) just needs to match what we verified:\n\n- swap `FlareDSDataset` \u2192 `FilamentDataset`\n- swap the flare-specific kwargs (`ds_flare_index_path=cfg.data.flare_index_path`) \u2192 the filament ones (`filament_index_path=cfg.data.filament_index_path`, `ds_time_column`, `ds_time_tolerance`, `ds_match_direction`)\n- point `cfg.data.train_data_path` / `cfg.data.valid_data_path` at `surya_index_filament_events.csv` \u2014 for now that has to happen as an in-notebook override on `cfg` (same as my test script), since we're holding off on editing `config_script.yaml`\n\nOne thing worth deciding before I put this in the notebook: right now train and val are the **same 11-row file**, so `build_helio_dataloaders` gives you two datasets with the exact same 11 events \u2014 not a real split, just harmless duplication (per the earlier discussion, a coin-flip baseline doesn't need a train/val split at all, since nothing is trained). Do you want the notebook to:\n\n1. Keep it as-is (train == val == all 11, matching what we just tested), and just use one of them (or both, ignoring the redundancy) for the coin-flip accuracy calculation, or\n2. Build a single dataset (skip `build_helio_dataloaders`'s train/val split entirely) since there's no training happening, or\n3. Something else (e.g. actually split the 11 events into two groups)?Short answer: yes, if you actually fine-tune Surya, you'll want *some* form of held-out evaluation \u2014 but at N=45 (and 11 today), a conventional fixed train/val/test split is probably the wrong tool.\n\n**Why the customary 3-way split exists:** train to fit weights, val to pick checkpoints/hyperparameters without touching test, test for one final unbiased number. That logic doesn't go away just because the dataset is small \u2014 if you skip held-out evaluation entirely, you have no way to tell \"the model learned something real\" from \"the model memorized the training set,\" which is exactly the failure mode LoRA/fine-tuning on 45 samples is prone to.\n\n**Why a fixed split is risky here:** with 45 events, a customary split (say ~30/8/7) leaves a test set small enough that flipping one or two predictions swings accuracy by 15-30 percentage points. A single number from a set that small isn't trustworthy on its own \u2014 you could get a great or terrible score by luck of which events landed in test.\n\n**What's more standard at this scale:** k-fold cross-validation (or leave-one-out, given how small N is) rather than one fixed split. Every event serves as test exactly once across folds, val is carved out of each fold's training portion for checkpoint selection, and you report an aggregate accuracy with a spread \u2014 not one point estimate. That also composes naturally with the coin-flip Monte Carlo distribution we discussed earlier: both give you \"a distribution to compare against\" instead of \"a single number,\" which is the more honest comparison anyway given N.\n\nThis is purely a training-time design question \u2014 it doesn't affect the coin-flip baseline at all, since that has no train/val/test distinction (no fitting happens). Worth deciding once you're actually building the fine-tuning notebook, not now.A few other cheap, well-established baselines worth considering alongside coin-flip:\n\n- **Majority-class baseline** \u2014 always predict whichever chirality is more common in the (training portion of the) catalog. It's the standard classification-baseline partner to coin-flip: coin-flip tells you \"chance with no information,\" majority-class tells you \"chance exploiting only the label imbalance.\" If your 45 events skew toward one chirality (your current 11 lean heavily dextral, 9/11), this could already beat 50% without looking at a single pixel \u2014 worth knowing so Surya isn't just rediscovering the imbalance.\n\n- **Logistic regression on hemisphere alone** \u2014 a \"calibrated\" version of Martin's Rule. Instead of assuming the textbook ~90% accuracy from the literature, you fit `P(chirality | hemisphere)` directly from your own catalog. With a single binary feature and N this small, that's just two conditional rates, but it's honest about *this* dataset's actual hemisphere/chirality association \u2014 including however much it's been diluted by the exception events you specifically included \u2014 rather than importing an external number that may not hold for a curated sample. This gives you the useful comparison Martin's Rule was meant to provide, without the \"declined it because it's unfairly strong for this catalog\" problem, since it's fit to the same exceptions.\n\n- **Martin's Rule itself, reported but not as the primary baseline** \u2014 you already decided against using it as *the* number to beat, but there's a case for computing and printing it anyway, next to coin-flip: seeing how far your catalog's actual hemisphere-chirality agreement has drifted from the textbook 90% is itself informative context, even if it's not the bar Surya needs to clear.\n\n- **Persistence** (repeat the previous event's label / same active region tends to repeat chirality) \u2014 this is the classic third option in the template's own baseline philosophy, but it's a poor fit here unless your catalog has multiple filaments from the same active region or recurrence \u2014 worth skipping unless that's true of your data.\n\nMy take: coin-flip (floor) + majority-class (imbalance-only) + hemisphere logistic regression (calibrated Martin's Rule) together give a clean three-point spectrum \u2014 no information, label-only information, and cheap physical prior \u2014 and all three are trivial to compute at N=45 with no real overfitting risk. Want me to add majority-class and/or the hemisphere logistic regression into the notebook alongside coin-flip?",
