      "time": 1789569722.135863,
      "type": "msg",
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "889318f7-d5ee-45eb-8c75-ad2ed5b12784",
      "body": "I think the calibrated version of Martin's Rule is reasonable to include. The high accuracy for chirality given hemisphere is generally true for a large number of events, but since my catalog has lots of exceptions, the calibrated Martin's Rule could be more realistic about the input."
    },
    {
      "time": 1789569743.7008758,
      "body": "Good call \u2014 that's the more honest use of it given your catalog's composition. One methodological detail worth flagging before we implement it: if we estimate `P(chirality | hemisphere)` from all 11 (eventually 45) events and then score accuracy on those same events, that's in-sample \u2014 the \"calibration\" would be partly fitting to the exceptions it's then graded on, which flatters the baseline the same way naive Martin's Rule can mislead in the other direction.\n\nThe fix is the same leave-one-out idea we discussed for eventual model evaluation: for each event, compute the hemisphere\u2192chirality rates from the *other* 10 (or 44) events, predict the held-out one from that, then aggregate accuracy across all leave-one-out folds. At this N, that's cheap \u2014 no actual model fitting, just per-hemisphere counts recomputed each fold \u2014 and it means the calibrated Martin's Rule is evaluated the same honest way Surya eventually will be.\n\nWant me to implement both now \u2014 coin-flip (Monte Carlo distribution) and this leave-one-out calibrated Martin's Rule \u2014 in the notebook?",
