    {
      "time": 1789572115.2638264,
      "type": "msg",
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Both baselines are now appended as new cells at the end of `1_baseline_template.ipynb`, after the \"Conclusion\" cell \u2014 a markdown intro, a setup cell (loads the correct filament config, overrides `train_data_path`/`valid_data_path` in-memory to the app-local `surya_index_filament_events.csv`, builds `FilamentDataset` with `return_surya_stack=False`), then the coin-flip Monte Carlo baseline and the leave-one-out calibrated Martin's Rule baseline, each with a markdown explanation, plus a summary cell.\n\nI couldn't get the notebook's live kernel to execute via the Jupyter MCP tool in this session (`run_cell` returned without producing outputs even after waiting), so I verified the exact same code standalone in the `surya_ws` conda environment instead. Results:\n\n- 11/11 catalog events matched (9 dextral, 2 sinistral; 4 south, 7 north)\n- **Coin-flip (Monte Carlo, 100k trials):** mean accuracy 0.500, std 0.150, 5th\u201395th percentile 0.273\u20130.727\n- **Calibrated Martin's Rule (leave-one-out):** 0.818 accuracy (9/11)\n\nThe LOO result is close to the plain majority-class rate here because dextral dominates in *both* hemispheres in this catalog \u2014 a direct illustration of why the textbook ~90% rule doesn't hold on this particular exception-laden set. You'll want to re-run the new cells in your live notebook session to get outputs saved in the file itself.",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "id": "a8fc182d-29b4-4e77-a314-6f083b6f9131"
    },
    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg",
